.TH FKSPRINTF 3 2026-09-24 "fkstring" "fkstring Library Manual"
.nh
.SH NAME
fksprintf, fkvsprintf, fkstrcatf, fkstrcatvf \- formatted output to an fkstring
.SH LIBRARY
fkstring library
.RI ( libfkstring ,\~ \-lfkstring )
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <fkstring.h>
.PP
.BI "fkstring *fksprintf(const char *" fmt ", ...);"
.BI "fkstring *fkvsprintf(const char *" fmt ", va_list " ap );
.PP
.BI "fkstring *fkstrcatf(fkstring *" dst ", const char *" fmt ", ...);"
.BI "fkstring *fkstrcatvf(fkstring *" dst ", const char *" fmt ", va_list " ap );
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
These functions format their arguments under the control of
.I fmt
as
.BR printf (3)
does.
.BR fksprintf ()
and
.BR fkvsprintf ()
return the output as a new
.BR fkstring .
.BR fkstrcatf ()
and
.BR fkstrcatvf ()
append it to
.I dst
in place.
.PP
The output is written directly into
.IR dst 's
buffer, after its current contents.
If it fits in the space already allocated, nothing is reallocated.
Otherwise
.I dst
grows once, by the bump factor described in
.BR fkstring (3),
and the output is formatted a second time.
Formatting into an empty string starts from a 48-byte guess
.RI ( _sprintftry ).
.PP
A
.B %c
conversion with a zero argument produces an embedded NUL byte, which is
counted in the length.
.PP
.BR fkvsprintf ()
and
.BR fkstrcatvf ()
take a
.I va_list
instead of a variable number of arguments, for writing variadic wrappers.
As with
.BR vprintf (3),
the value of
.I ap
is indeterminate after the call; pass it to
.BR va_end (3)
and do not use it again.
.SH RETURN VALUE
.BR fksprintf ()
and
.BR fkvsprintf ()
return a new
.BR fkstring ,
or NULL if
.I fmt
is NULL.
.PP
.BR fkstrcatf ()
and
.BR fkstrcatvf ()
return
.IR dst ,
or NULL, leaving
.I dst
unchanged, if
.I dst
or
.I fmt
is NULL.
.SH ERRORS
These functions do not return on allocation failure; see
.BR fkstring (3).
.SH BUGS
If
.BR vsnprintf (3)
itself fails, these functions terminate the process with the message
.RB \(dq "Inconsistency in vsnprintf() behavior" \(dq
instead of returning an error.
This happens when the output would exceed
.B INT_MAX
bytes, or when a
.B %ls
or
.B %lc
argument cannot be converted in the current locale.
.PP
With glibc versions before 2.1, whose
.BR vsnprintf (3)
does not report the length needed, output more than ten times longer
than the first guess fails in the same way.
.SH EXAMPLES
.EX
static void
logmsg(fkstring *log, const char *fmt, ...)
{
    va_list ap;
\&
    fkstrcatc(log, "[log] ");
    va_start(ap, fmt);
    fkstrcatvf(log, fmt, ap);
    va_end(ap);
    fkstrcatone(log, \(aq\en\(aq);
}
\&
fkstring *s = fksprintf("%d items", 3);
fkstrcatf(s, " at %.2f each", 1.5);   /* "3 items at 1.50 each" */
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR printf (3),
.BR fkstring (3),
.BR fkstrcat (3)
